1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,381
Imagine two people.

2
00:00:01,561 --> 00:00:03,395
Both of them are turning thirty.

3
00:00:03,575 --> 00:00:06,559
One turns thirty in nineteen sixty-two.

4
00:00:06,739 --> 00:00:09,435
The other turns thirty this year,

5
00:00:09,435 --> 00:00:11,151
in twenty twenty-six.

6
00:00:11,331 --> 00:00:12,631
They're the same age,

7
00:00:12,631 --> 00:00:14,489
maybe even from the same town.

8
00:00:14,669 --> 00:00:16,855
But they are living in two completely

9
00:00:16,855 --> 00:00:17,978
different Americas.

10
00:00:18,327 --> 00:00:21,260
The one in nineteen sixty-two was born

11
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:23,035
in nineteen thirty-two,

12
00:00:23,035 --> 00:00:25,967
in the depths of the Great Depression.

13
00:00:26,147 --> 00:00:26,991
As a child,

14
00:00:26,991 --> 00:00:29,216
he heard about the war on the

15
00:00:29,216 --> 00:00:29,676
radio.

16
00:00:29,856 --> 00:00:30,934
As a young man,

17
00:00:30,934 --> 00:00:32,945
he may have served in Korea.

18
00:00:33,125 --> 00:00:33,935
And by thirty,

19
00:00:33,935 --> 00:00:35,323
he is already a husband,

20
00:00:35,323 --> 00:00:36,770
a father and a homeowner.

21
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:39,315
So let's put them side by side.

22
00:00:39,495 --> 00:00:39,750
Home.

23
00:00:39,930 --> 00:00:40,197
Work.

24
00:00:40,377 --> 00:00:40,900
Money.

25
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:41,753
Marriage.

26
00:00:41,933 --> 00:00:42,630
Children.

27
00:00:42,810 --> 00:00:43,181
Health.

28
00:00:43,361 --> 00:00:45,317
And the things that don't show up

29
00:00:45,317 --> 00:00:46,740
in any statistic at all.

30
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:48,635
And then let's try to answer an

31
00:00:48,635 --> 00:00:49,520
honest question.

32
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:51,825
Who actually had it better at thirty?

33
00:00:52,175 --> 00:00:54,566
And the thirty-year-old of twenty twenty-six?

34
00:00:54,746 --> 00:00:57,393
Born in nineteen ninety-six.

35
00:00:57,573 --> 00:01:00,139
A child when the twin towers fell.

36
00:01:00,319 --> 00:01:02,517
A teenager in the middle of the

37
00:01:02,517 --> 00:01:03,651
great recession.

38
00:01:03,831 --> 00:01:06,331
Starting a career just as a pandemic

39
00:01:06,331 --> 00:01:07,720
shut the world down.

40
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:09,707
Two very different childhoods,

41
00:01:09,707 --> 00:01:12,719
leading to two very different thirtieth birthdays.

42
00:01:13,069 --> 00:01:14,647
Let's start with the house.

43
00:01:14,827 --> 00:01:16,253
In the early sixties,

44
00:01:16,253 --> 00:01:18,764
the typical new house sold for around

45
00:01:18,764 --> 00:01:20,528
eighteen thousand dollars.

46
00:01:20,708 --> 00:01:23,793
A typical family earned around six thousand

47
00:01:23,793 --> 00:01:24,295
a year.

48
00:01:24,475 --> 00:01:26,971
So a home cost roughly three years

49
00:01:26,971 --> 00:01:29,832
of income, and a thirty-year-old with a

50
00:01:29,832 --> 00:01:32,254
steady job could usually buy one.

51
00:01:32,605 --> 00:01:35,071
Today, the typical new house sells for

52
00:01:35,071 --> 00:01:37,473
around four hundred thousand dollars,

53
00:01:37,473 --> 00:01:40,265
and a typical household earns around eighty

54
00:01:40,265 --> 00:01:41,823
thousand dollars a year.

55
00:01:42,003 --> 00:01:44,379
So a home costs about five years

56
00:01:44,379 --> 00:01:47,349
of income, before you even count today's

57
00:01:47,349 --> 00:01:49,503
interest rates and insurance.

58
00:01:49,683 --> 00:01:52,275
That gap is the single biggest difference

59
00:01:52,275 --> 00:01:53,793
between these two lives.

60
00:01:54,143 --> 00:01:55,751
In nineteen sixty-two,

61
00:01:55,751 --> 00:01:58,383
the average age of a first-time home

62
00:01:58,383 --> 00:02:00,284
buyer was in the twenties.

63
00:02:00,464 --> 00:02:02,833
Today, it's well into the thirties.

64
00:02:03,013 --> 00:02:06,521
Our thirty-year-old in nineteen sixty-two has a

65
00:02:06,521 --> 00:02:08,760
mortgage, a lawn and a garage.

66
00:02:08,940 --> 00:02:11,768
A great many thirty-year-olds today are still

67
00:02:11,768 --> 00:02:14,219
renting, and some are living with their

68
00:02:14,219 --> 00:02:14,721
parents.

69
00:02:15,072 --> 00:02:17,765
But look closer at the two houses.

70
00:02:17,945 --> 00:02:20,896
The nineteen sixty-two house was small,

71
00:02:20,896 --> 00:02:23,998
often around a thousand or twelve hundred

72
00:02:23,998 --> 00:02:26,873
square feet, with one bathroom for the

73
00:02:26,873 --> 00:02:29,446
whole family, no air conditioning,

74
00:02:29,446 --> 00:02:31,262
and a single-car garage.

75
00:02:31,442 --> 00:02:34,161
The typical new house today is about

76
00:02:34,161 --> 00:02:35,369
twice that size,

77
00:02:35,369 --> 00:02:37,861
with central air and two or three

78
00:02:37,861 --> 00:02:38,617
bathrooms.

79
00:02:38,967 --> 00:02:43,100
The children in nineteen sixty-two usually shared

80
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:45,885
bedrooms, two or three to a room,

81
00:02:45,885 --> 00:02:47,825
sometimes in bunk beds.

82
00:02:48,005 --> 00:02:50,065
There was one telephone,

83
00:02:50,065 --> 00:02:52,382
one television and one car.

84
00:02:52,562 --> 00:02:55,174
The idea that every child would have

85
00:02:55,174 --> 00:02:56,988
a room of their own was a

86
00:02:56,988 --> 00:02:58,947
luxury, not an expectation.

87
00:02:59,127 --> 00:03:01,171
And yet nobody felt poor.

88
00:03:01,351 --> 00:03:03,719
Everybody they knew lived the same way.

89
00:03:04,070 --> 00:03:06,275
Getting the mortgage was simpler too.

90
00:03:06,455 --> 00:03:08,575
You went to the local bank or

91
00:03:08,575 --> 00:03:09,817
savings and loan,

92
00:03:09,817 --> 00:03:11,937
sat down with a man who might

93
00:03:11,937 --> 00:03:13,179
know your father,

94
00:03:13,179 --> 00:03:15,372
and showed him your pay stubs.

95
00:03:15,552 --> 00:03:18,491
Many veterans bought with almost no money

96
00:03:18,491 --> 00:03:20,498
down, thanks to the GI Bill.

97
00:03:20,678 --> 00:03:22,771
A handshake and a signature,

98
00:03:22,771 --> 00:03:24,939
and a few weeks later you had

99
00:03:24,939 --> 00:03:25,612
the keys.

100
00:03:25,962 --> 00:03:27,733
In nineteen sixty-two,

101
00:03:27,733 --> 00:03:30,953
a thirty-year-old man with a high school

102
00:03:30,953 --> 00:03:33,771
diploma could find steady work in a

103
00:03:33,771 --> 00:03:35,381
factory, a railroad,

104
00:03:35,381 --> 00:03:37,716
a utility company or a store,

105
00:03:37,716 --> 00:03:40,695
and expect to stay there for decades.

106
00:03:40,875 --> 00:03:42,989
Many jobs came with a pension that

107
00:03:42,989 --> 00:03:45,042
would pay him every month for the

108
00:03:45,042 --> 00:03:46,099
rest of his life.

109
00:03:46,449 --> 00:03:50,325
Today, the thirty-year-old has probably had five

110
00:03:50,325 --> 00:03:51,940
or six jobs already.

111
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:54,198
Many of the factories are gone.

112
00:03:54,378 --> 00:03:57,741
Pensions have mostly been replaced by retirement

113
00:03:57,741 --> 00:04:00,613
accounts that depend on the stock market.

114
00:04:00,793 --> 00:04:02,117
On the other hand,

115
00:04:02,117 --> 00:04:05,207
there are careers today that simply didn't

116
00:04:05,207 --> 00:04:07,266
exist in nineteen sixty-two,

117
00:04:07,266 --> 00:04:09,547
and many of them pay very well.

118
00:04:09,897 --> 00:04:12,880
For women, the difference is enormous.

119
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:14,672
In nineteen sixty-two,

120
00:04:14,672 --> 00:04:18,115
a thirty-year-old woman was usually expected to

121
00:04:18,115 --> 00:04:20,166
stay home with her children.

122
00:04:20,346 --> 00:04:21,397
If she worked,

123
00:04:21,397 --> 00:04:24,628
her choices were often limited to teaching,

124
00:04:24,628 --> 00:04:27,332
nursing, secretarial work or retail,

125
00:04:27,332 --> 00:04:29,887
and she was usually paid much less

126
00:04:29,887 --> 00:04:31,990
than a man for the same job.

127
00:04:32,170 --> 00:04:34,754
Today, women make up nearly half of

128
00:04:34,754 --> 00:04:37,337
the workforce, in every profession.

129
00:04:37,687 --> 00:04:39,348
In nineteen sixty-two,

130
00:04:39,348 --> 00:04:42,521
one paycheck was usually enough to support

131
00:04:42,521 --> 00:04:43,805
the whole family.

132
00:04:43,985 --> 00:04:46,845
Today, in most families with children,

133
00:04:46,845 --> 00:04:48,199
both parents work.

134
00:04:48,379 --> 00:04:51,138
Two incomes are often needed to cover

135
00:04:51,138 --> 00:04:53,674
what one income covered back then.

136
00:04:53,854 --> 00:04:56,115
That's one of the hardest facts about

137
00:04:56,115 --> 00:04:58,927
the comparison, and almost everybody feels it.

138
00:04:59,278 --> 00:05:01,957
The work week in nineteen sixty-two was

139
00:05:01,957 --> 00:05:04,636
forty hours, and when the whistle blew,

140
00:05:04,636 --> 00:05:05,872
the work was over.

141
00:05:06,052 --> 00:05:07,979
Nobody called you at dinner.

142
00:05:08,159 --> 00:05:10,244
Nobody sent you a message at ten

143
00:05:10,244 --> 00:05:11,352
o'clock at night.

144
00:05:11,532 --> 00:05:15,049
Today's thirty-year-old may earn more in real

145
00:05:15,049 --> 00:05:16,612
terms, but for many,

146
00:05:16,612 --> 00:05:18,800
the work never really stops.

147
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:20,930
It follows them home in their pocket.

148
00:05:21,281 --> 00:05:23,068
Now the everyday prices.

149
00:05:23,248 --> 00:05:25,222
In nineteen sixty-two,

150
00:05:25,222 --> 00:05:28,271
a gallon of gas cost around thirty

151
00:05:28,271 --> 00:05:28,810
cents.

152
00:05:28,990 --> 00:05:31,451
A loaf of bread around twenty cents.

153
00:05:31,631 --> 00:05:34,670
A new car around twenty-five hundred dollars,

154
00:05:34,670 --> 00:05:36,899
which was less than half a year's

155
00:05:36,899 --> 00:05:37,169
pay.

156
00:05:37,349 --> 00:05:40,139
Today, the average new car costs well

157
00:05:40,139 --> 00:05:42,251
over forty thousand dollars,

158
00:05:42,251 --> 00:05:44,815
more than half a year's income for

159
00:05:44,815 --> 00:05:46,323
a typical household.

160
00:05:46,674 --> 00:05:49,341
But many things are far cheaper today,

161
00:05:49,341 --> 00:05:51,167
measured in hours of work.

162
00:05:51,347 --> 00:05:54,813
A television in nineteen sixty-two could cost

163
00:05:54,813 --> 00:05:56,816
two or three weeks of pay,

164
00:05:56,816 --> 00:05:59,667
and it showed three channels in black

165
00:05:59,667 --> 00:06:00,437
and white.

166
00:06:00,617 --> 00:06:04,795
Clothing, appliances, a long-distance phone call,

167
00:06:04,795 --> 00:06:06,074
a plane ticket.

168
00:06:06,254 --> 00:06:08,797
Many of those cost a working family

169
00:06:08,797 --> 00:06:11,049
far more back then than they do

170
00:06:11,049 --> 00:06:11,339
now.

171
00:06:11,689 --> 00:06:13,918
And health care was a different world.

172
00:06:14,098 --> 00:06:15,599
In nineteen sixty-two,

173
00:06:15,599 --> 00:06:17,783
a visit to the doctor might cost

174
00:06:17,783 --> 00:06:20,104
five dollars, and he might come to

175
00:06:20,104 --> 00:06:20,855
your house.

176
00:06:21,035 --> 00:06:22,902
But there was much less that medicine

177
00:06:22,902 --> 00:06:23,357
could do.

178
00:06:23,537 --> 00:06:25,557
Medicare didn't exist yet.

179
00:06:25,737 --> 00:06:27,594
Many hospitals were small.

180
00:06:27,774 --> 00:06:30,947
And plenty of illnesses that are treatable

181
00:06:30,947 --> 00:06:33,591
today were simply a matter of luck.

182
00:06:33,941 --> 00:06:36,731
Life expectancy for a baby born in

183
00:06:36,731 --> 00:06:40,670
America in nineteen sixty-two was around seventy

184
00:06:40,670 --> 00:06:41,162
years.

185
00:06:41,342 --> 00:06:43,397
Today it's close to seventy-nine.

186
00:06:43,577 --> 00:06:47,348
Our thirty-year-old in twenty twenty-six can expect

187
00:06:47,348 --> 00:06:48,458
to live longer,

188
00:06:48,458 --> 00:06:50,750
and to be healthier in old age,

189
00:06:50,750 --> 00:06:54,152
than almost anyone in nineteen sixty-two could

190
00:06:54,152 --> 00:06:55,187
have imagined.

191
00:06:55,537 --> 00:06:58,892
Smoking was everywhere in nineteen sixty-two.

192
00:06:59,072 --> 00:07:01,577
More than four out of ten American

193
00:07:01,577 --> 00:07:03,494
adults smoked, in offices,

194
00:07:03,494 --> 00:07:05,483
on airplanes, in hospitals,

195
00:07:05,483 --> 00:07:07,768
even in doctors' waiting rooms.

196
00:07:07,948 --> 00:07:11,289
The Surgeon General's famous report on smoking

197
00:07:11,289 --> 00:07:14,195
and health didn't come out until January

198
00:07:14,195 --> 00:07:15,866
of nineteen sixty-four.

199
00:07:16,046 --> 00:07:18,881
Today, only about one adult in nine

200
00:07:18,881 --> 00:07:19,447
smokes.

201
00:07:19,798 --> 00:07:21,427
In nineteen sixty-two,

202
00:07:21,427 --> 00:07:24,908
the typical man married at around twenty-three,

203
00:07:24,908 --> 00:07:27,797
and the typical woman at around twenty.

204
00:07:27,977 --> 00:07:30,224
By thirty, most of them had been

205
00:07:30,224 --> 00:07:32,823
married for several years and had two

206
00:07:32,823 --> 00:07:34,087
or three children,

207
00:07:34,087 --> 00:07:35,140
sometimes more.

208
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:37,979
The baby boom was still going strong.

209
00:07:38,329 --> 00:07:40,599
Today, the typical age at a first

210
00:07:40,599 --> 00:07:43,145
marriage is around thirty for men and

211
00:07:43,145 --> 00:07:45,759
twenty-eight or twenty-nine for women.

212
00:07:45,939 --> 00:07:49,098
So our thirty-year-old in twenty twenty-six may

213
00:07:49,098 --> 00:07:50,712
just be getting married,

214
00:07:50,712 --> 00:07:52,661
or may not be married at all.

215
00:07:52,841 --> 00:07:55,416
Many are waiting longer to have children,

216
00:07:55,416 --> 00:07:56,986
and having fewer of them.

217
00:07:57,336 --> 00:07:59,576
Raising children looked different too.

218
00:07:59,756 --> 00:08:01,625
In nineteen sixty-two,

219
00:08:01,625 --> 00:08:05,701
kids played outside all day without supervision.

220
00:08:05,881 --> 00:08:08,239
Mothers sent them out after breakfast and

221
00:08:08,239 --> 00:08:09,735
called them in for supper.

222
00:08:09,915 --> 00:08:13,180
Today, children are watched far more closely,

223
00:08:13,180 --> 00:08:14,993
driven to every activity,

224
00:08:14,993 --> 00:08:17,822
and kept busy with schedules that would

225
00:08:17,822 --> 00:08:20,724
have amazed a nineteen sixty-two mother.

226
00:08:21,074 --> 00:08:23,117
And the cost of raising them has

227
00:08:23,117 --> 00:08:24,394
changed most of all.

228
00:08:24,574 --> 00:08:26,141
In nineteen sixty-two,

229
00:08:26,141 --> 00:08:28,848
a family with three children could get

230
00:08:28,848 --> 00:08:30,060
by on one income,

231
00:08:30,060 --> 00:08:33,265
with hand-me-down clothes and a state college

232
00:08:33,265 --> 00:08:35,830
costing a few hundred dollars a year

233
00:08:35,830 --> 00:08:36,614
in tuition.

234
00:08:36,794 --> 00:08:39,933
Today, child care and college tuition are

235
00:08:39,933 --> 00:08:43,149
among the biggest worries any young family

236
00:08:43,149 --> 00:08:43,609
faces.

237
00:08:43,959 --> 00:08:47,567
Most thirty-year-olds in nineteen sixty-two had not

238
00:08:47,567 --> 00:08:49,195
gone to college at all.

239
00:08:49,375 --> 00:08:51,885
Only about one adult in twelve had

240
00:08:51,885 --> 00:08:53,287
a four-year degree.

241
00:08:53,467 --> 00:08:55,921
A high school diploma was enough to

242
00:08:55,921 --> 00:08:57,182
build a good life.

243
00:08:57,362 --> 00:08:59,544
Today, more than a third of adults

244
00:08:59,544 --> 00:09:01,148
have a bachelor's degree,

245
00:09:01,148 --> 00:09:03,842
and many young people start their thirties

246
00:09:03,842 --> 00:09:06,023
still paying off the loans for it.

247
00:09:06,374 --> 00:09:09,706
A typical evening in nineteen sixty-two.

248
00:09:09,886 --> 00:09:10,960
Dinner at six,

249
00:09:10,960 --> 00:09:13,647
with the whole family at the table.

250
00:09:13,827 --> 00:09:15,697
The news at six-thirty.

251
00:09:15,877 --> 00:09:18,384
A game show or a western at

252
00:09:18,384 --> 00:09:18,942
seven.

253
00:09:19,122 --> 00:09:21,432
The children in bed by eight-thirty.

254
00:09:21,612 --> 00:09:23,714
The parents on the porch or in

255
00:09:23,714 --> 00:09:25,395
front of the television,

256
00:09:25,395 --> 00:09:27,777
maybe with a neighbor stopping by.

257
00:09:27,957 --> 00:09:29,005
And the phone,

258
00:09:29,005 --> 00:09:29,904
on the wall,

259
00:09:29,904 --> 00:09:31,626
rarely rang after nine.

260
00:09:31,976 --> 00:09:35,064
A typical evening in twenty twenty-six.

261
00:09:35,244 --> 00:09:37,786
Dinner, if everybody's home at the same

262
00:09:37,786 --> 00:09:38,112
time.

263
00:09:38,292 --> 00:09:40,350
Then every member of the family on

264
00:09:40,350 --> 00:09:41,501
a different screen,

265
00:09:41,501 --> 00:09:42,712
in a different room,

266
00:09:42,712 --> 00:09:44,468
watching something different.

267
00:09:44,648 --> 00:09:48,119
More choices than anyone in nineteen sixty-two

268
00:09:48,119 --> 00:09:49,176
could imagine.

269
00:09:49,356 --> 00:09:51,794
But often, a lot less together.

270
00:09:52,144 --> 00:09:53,643
In nineteen sixty-two,

271
00:09:53,643 --> 00:09:56,846
our thirty-year-old knew his neighbors by name.

272
00:09:57,026 --> 00:09:59,147
He belonged to a church,

273
00:09:59,147 --> 00:10:00,384
a union local,

274
00:10:00,384 --> 00:10:01,887
a bowling league,

275
00:10:01,887 --> 00:10:04,273
or a lodge like the Elks or

276
00:10:04,273 --> 00:10:05,245
the Rotary.

277
00:10:05,425 --> 00:10:08,762
Membership in clubs and community groups was

278
00:10:08,762 --> 00:10:10,278
at an all-time high.

279
00:10:10,458 --> 00:10:12,623
That kind of belonging is much harder

280
00:10:12,623 --> 00:10:13,442
to find today.

281
00:10:13,793 --> 00:10:16,314
Shopping was a trip into town,

282
00:10:16,314 --> 00:10:17,322
with a list.

283
00:10:17,502 --> 00:10:19,523
If a store didn't have what you

284
00:10:19,523 --> 00:10:22,066
needed, you ordered it from the catalog

285
00:10:22,066 --> 00:10:23,435
and waited two weeks.

286
00:10:23,615 --> 00:10:27,095
Today's thirty-year-old can have almost anything delivered

287
00:10:27,095 --> 00:10:28,595
to the door the next day.

288
00:10:28,775 --> 00:10:31,239
That's a kind of convenience no one

289
00:10:31,239 --> 00:10:34,337
in nineteen sixty-two could have dreamed of.

290
00:10:34,687 --> 00:10:36,625
Vacations were simpler too.

291
00:10:36,805 --> 00:10:38,270
A week at the lake,

292
00:10:38,270 --> 00:10:40,505
or a road trip in the station

293
00:10:40,505 --> 00:10:42,972
wagon to see the Grand Canyon or

294
00:10:42,972 --> 00:10:44,282
the grandparents.

295
00:10:44,462 --> 00:10:46,772
Flying was a luxury for the wealthy,

296
00:10:46,772 --> 00:10:48,889
and people dressed up to board an

297
00:10:48,889 --> 00:10:49,466
airplane.

298
00:10:49,646 --> 00:10:52,226
Today a young couple can fly across

299
00:10:52,226 --> 00:10:54,733
the country for less than a week's

300
00:10:54,733 --> 00:10:55,765
pay, in jeans.

301
00:10:56,115 --> 00:10:58,616
And yet the nineteen sixty-two world had

302
00:10:58,616 --> 00:10:59,992
real fears of its own.

303
00:11:00,172 --> 00:11:03,081
That autumn, during the Cuban Missile Crisis,

304
00:11:03,081 --> 00:11:05,861
families listened to the radio and wondered

305
00:11:05,861 --> 00:11:07,347
if the world would end.

306
00:11:07,527 --> 00:11:10,650
Schoolchildren practiced hiding under their desks.

307
00:11:10,830 --> 00:11:13,774
Every generation of thirty-year-olds has its own

308
00:11:13,774 --> 00:11:15,614
reasons to lie awake at night.

309
00:11:15,964 --> 00:11:17,426
So what's the verdict?

310
00:11:17,606 --> 00:11:19,201
In nineteen sixty-two,

311
00:11:19,201 --> 00:11:21,159
the big things were easier.

312
00:11:21,339 --> 00:11:23,155
A house, a car and a family

313
00:11:23,155 --> 00:11:25,577
were within reach for a young couple

314
00:11:25,577 --> 00:11:27,864
with ordinary jobs and one income.

315
00:11:28,044 --> 00:11:29,460
Communities were tight.

316
00:11:29,640 --> 00:11:31,473
The future felt like it was going

317
00:11:31,473 --> 00:11:32,972
to be better than the past.

318
00:11:33,323 --> 00:11:34,991
In twenty twenty-six,

319
00:11:34,991 --> 00:11:37,851
the small things are easier and life

320
00:11:37,851 --> 00:11:38,884
lasts longer.

321
00:11:39,064 --> 00:11:40,515
Medicine is better.

322
00:11:40,695 --> 00:11:42,309
Food is more varied.

323
00:11:42,489 --> 00:11:43,998
Travel is cheaper.

324
00:11:44,178 --> 00:11:47,173
Women and minorities have choices that were

325
00:11:47,173 --> 00:11:49,751
closed to them in nineteen sixty-two.

326
00:11:49,931 --> 00:11:51,189
But the big things,

327
00:11:51,189 --> 00:11:52,977
the house and the security,

328
00:11:52,977 --> 00:11:54,633
are much harder to reach.

329
00:11:54,983 --> 00:11:57,293
And some things simply can't be compared.

330
00:11:57,473 --> 00:12:01,029
A nineteen sixty-two thirty-year-old who was Black,

331
00:12:01,029 --> 00:12:02,633
in much of the country,

332
00:12:02,633 --> 00:12:05,422
could not eat at certain lunch counters,

333
00:12:05,422 --> 00:12:07,514
vote freely, or buy a house in

334
00:12:07,514 --> 00:12:09,048
certain neighborhoods.

335
00:12:09,228 --> 00:12:10,865
For millions of Americans,

336
00:12:10,865 --> 00:12:12,816
the good old days were not good

337
00:12:12,816 --> 00:12:13,257
at all.

338
00:12:13,437 --> 00:12:15,445
That's part of the honest answer too.

339
00:12:15,796 --> 00:12:18,268
Maybe the honest answer is this.

340
00:12:18,448 --> 00:12:21,875
The thirty-year-old of nineteen sixty-two had less,

341
00:12:21,875 --> 00:12:23,487
but was more sure of it.

342
00:12:23,667 --> 00:12:26,255
The thirty-year-old of today has more,

343
00:12:26,255 --> 00:12:28,026
but is less sure of all of

344
00:12:28,026 --> 00:12:28,230
it.

345
00:12:28,410 --> 00:12:30,169
And if you were thirty in the

346
00:12:30,169 --> 00:12:31,988
sixties or seventies yourself,

347
00:12:31,988 --> 00:12:34,110
you probably have your own opinion.

348
00:12:34,461 --> 00:12:36,388
So tell us in the comments.

349
00:12:36,568 --> 00:12:38,489
How old were you when you bought

350
00:12:38,489 --> 00:12:39,510
your first house,

351
00:12:39,510 --> 00:12:40,771
and what did it cost?

352
00:12:40,951 --> 00:12:42,214
And who do you think had it

353
00:12:42,214 --> 00:12:42,541
better?

354
00:12:42,721 --> 00:12:44,172
We read every comment.

355
00:12:44,352 --> 00:12:45,521
And stay with us,

356
00:12:45,521 --> 00:12:48,822
because next we'll learn the money-saving tricks

357
00:12:48,822 --> 00:12:52,328
that every grandmother used in nineteen fifty-five.

358
00:12:52,508 --> 00:12:54,323
Thank you for spending this time with

359
00:12:54,323 --> 00:12:54,471
me.
